WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.441
The steamer bound for America was called the General Grant and would take 12 days to cross the Pacific.

2
00:00:06.442 --> 00:00:16.288
Phileas Fogg was hoping they would reach San Francisco by December 2, New York by December 11, and London, England, by December 20.

3
00:00:16.289 --> 00:00:20.968
According to the wager, his trip was to conclude by December 21.

4
00:00:20.969 --> 00:00:26.519
Mr. Fogg's young female companion, Aouda, felt herself growing attached to her protector.

5
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:30.162
Mr. Fogg's silent but generous nature impressed her.

6
00:00:30.163 --> 00:00:35.935
The more time she spent with him, the more she began to feel emotions stronger than simple gratitude.

7
00:00:35.936 --> 00:00:40.858
Her new feelings, however, did not seem to have any effect on Mr. Fogg.

8
00:00:40.859 --> 00:00:45.048
Passepartout did not fail to notice the state of the lady's heart.

9
00:00:45.049 --> 00:00:49.231
She was now as impatient as Fogg was over any delay in their journey.

10
00:00:49.232 --> 00:00:53.923
He assured her that the most difficult part of the journey had already passed.

11
00:00:53.924 --> 00:01:01.415
A railway train from San Francisco to New York and a steamer from New York to Liverpool would bring them to the end of their incredible travels.

12
00:01:01.416 --> 00:01:08.676
Now that Fix was out of their way, no obstacles could slow their progress. At least, that's what Passepartout thought.

13
00:01:08.677 --> 00:01:12.235
Detective Fix, however, was also on the General Grant

14
00:01:12.236 --> 00:01:15.708
. And he had the warrant for Mr. Fogg's arrest in his

15
00:01:15.709 --> 00:01:19.616
hand. It had been waiting for him at the English consulate in

16
00:01:19.617 --> 00:01:23.926
Yokohama. Unfortunately, now that Fogg had left English territory, it was

17
00:01:23.927 --> 00:01:27.717
useless. Fogg could not be arrested outside English

18
00:01:27.718 --> 00:01:33.384
territories. Six days out of Yokohama, Passepartout came face-to-face with Fix on the forward deck. Without

19
00:01:33.385 --> 00:01:40.827
a word Passepartout grabbed Fix by the throat and began to punch him, to the amusement of a group of Americans close by. When

20
00:01:40.828 --> 00:01:48.703
Passepartout had finished, Fix stood up and looked at his adversary coldly. "Are you done?" he asked. "For

21
00:01:48.704 --> 00:01:52.601
now . . . yes," said Passepartout with satisfaction. "Then

22
00:01:52.602 --> 00:01:56.436
let me have a word with you," said Fix. "You

23
00:01:56.437 --> 00:02:00.227
have given me a thrashing, and I expected it. Up

24
00:02:00.228 --> 00:02:06.617
to this time, I have been Mr. Fogg's adversary, but now I am on his side." "Aha!"

25
00:02:06.618 --> 00:02:12.169
cried Passepartout. "You are convinced he is an honest man!" "No,"

26
00:02:12.170 --> 00:02:16.837
replied Fix. "I think he is a rascal! As

27
00:02:16.838 --> 00:02:24.692
long as Mr. Fogg was on English ground, it was my duty to detain him until the warrant for his arrest arrived. But

28
00:02:24.693 --> 00:02:33.031
now I want him back on English soil as quickly as possible, and I'll do what I can to help him reach it. Are we friends?" "Friends?

29
00:02:33.032 --> 00:02:39.472
No," replied Passepartout. "But allies perhaps." On

30
00:02:39.473 --> 00:02:44.148
December 2, the General Grant reached San Francisco. Mr.

31
00:02:44.149 --> 00:02:47.901
Fogg had neither gained nor lost a single day. It

32
00:02:47.902 --> 00:02:54.439
was seven o'clock in the morning when Mr. Fogg, Aouda, and Passepartout set foot on American soil. At

33
00:02:54.440 --> 00:03:00.052
the first news vendor he saw, Mr. Fogg inquired when the next train left for New York. The

34
00:03:00.053 --> 00:03:06.377
train was scheduled to leave at 6:00 p.m. so they had the entire day to spend in the California city. They

35
00:03:06.378 --> 00:03:11.574
hired a carriage and went to the International Hotel to dine. After

36
00:03:11.575 --> 00:03:17.768
breakfast Mr. Fogg and Aouda were about to leave for the passport office when Passepartout stopped them. "Perhaps

37
00:03:17.769 --> 00:03:23.651
I should purchase some rifles and revolvers while I am shopping, master?" asked Passepartout. He

38
00:03:23.652 --> 00:03:27.909
had heard stories of Indians attacking the trains. "I

39
00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:33.146
think it is a useless precaution, but do as you think best," replied Mr. Fogg. Mr.

40
00:03:33.147 --> 00:03:38.617
Fogg and Aouda had not walked two hundred steps, however, when they ran into Mr. Fix. The

41
00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:42.539
detective pretended to be wholly taken by surprise. Fix

42
00:03:42.540 --> 00:03:50.090
asked to accompany them on their walk around San Francisco, a request that Mr. Fogg readily granted. They

43
00:03:50.091 --> 00:03:55.351
soon found themselves on Montgomery Street, where a large crowd had collected. The

44
00:03:55.352 --> 00:04:02.220
crowd was marching down the street, cheering "Hooray for Camerfield!" and carrying large posters and banners. "It

45
00:04:02.221 --> 00:04:05.144
is a political meeting," said Mr. Fogg. "We

46
00:04:05.145 --> 00:04:09.770
had better not mingle in the crowd. There may be danger." "Yes,"

47
00:04:09.771 --> 00:04:15.331
returned Fix. "A political rally can easily turn violent." They

48
00:04:15.332 --> 00:04:20.352
headed toward a flight of stairs at the side of the street that would take them out of the crowd. Just

49
00:04:20.353 --> 00:04:27.296
then another group, chanting "Hooray for Mandiboy!" came up the street from the opposite direction. The

50
00:04:27.297 --> 00:04:33.871
cheers quickly turned to shouts, and the posters turned to weapons as the opposing sides assaulted each other. It

51
00:04:33.872 --> 00:04:39.046
was too late to escape—Mr. Fogg, Aouda, and Fix were caught in the middle. Phileas

52
00:04:39.047 --> 00:04:45.363
Fogg and Fix bravely protected their fair companion, jostling roughly with the fighters. A tough,

53
00:04:45.364 --> 00:04:50.621
muscular fellow with a red beard rushed at Mr. Fogg with clenched fists. Before Mr.

54
00:04:50.622 --> 00:04:58.796
Fogg could clench his own fists, however, Fix stepped in and received the punch instead. Fix fell to the ground. Mr. Fogg

55
00:04:58.797 --> 00:05:07.145
stepped over him to face his opponent, but other combatants had moved between them and he couldn't reach the bearded man. "Yankee!" called

56
00:05:07.146 --> 00:05:10.951
Mr. Fogg. "We will meet again!" "Englishman!" retorted

57
00:05:10.952 --> 00:05:15.048
the other. "Do you challenge me?" "I do!"

58
00:05:15.049 --> 00:05:19.921
said Mr. Fogg confidently. "What is your name?" "Colonel Stamp

59
00:05:19.922 --> 00:05:22.565
Proctor," said the man. "Mine is

60
00:05:22.566 --> 00:05:33.684
Phileas Fogg, and I promise you we will face each other again!" Then the crowd swept them apart. Passepartout's Guidebook

61
00:05:33.685 --> 00:05:38.146
It seems that Mr. Fogg makes friends wherever he goes . . . and enemies!

62
00:05:38.147 --> 00:05:42.041
I hope we don't meet Colonel Proctor again—or any Indians.

63
00:05:42.042 --> 00:05:45.446
Are you wondering why there are people called Indians in America?

64
00:05:45.447 --> 00:05:52.428
Well, let me explain. When the explorer Christopher Columbus first arrived in America, he thought he was in India.

65
00:05:52.429 --> 00:05:56.990
So when he encountered the Native Americans, he called them "Indians."

66
00:05:56.991 --> 00:06:03.155
Like Europeans and Asians, the group of people known as Native American consists of hundreds of different cultures.

67
00:06:03.156 --> 00:06:07.270
Two Native American tribes can differ as much as Asian people do.

68
00:06:07.271 --> 00:06:12.320
Native Americans have lived in North America long before Europeans invaded the continent.

69
00:06:12.321 --> 00:06:16.635
Since the arrival of Europeans, there has been a lot of fighting over land.

70
00:06:16.636 --> 00:06:24.197
So far we haven't seen any of these wars, but our journey isn't over yet!

